---
import Base from "@/layouts/Base.astro";
import StudioBar from "@/layouts/components/StudioBar.astro";
import SiteFooter from "@/layouts/components/SiteFooter.astro";
import "@/lib/prompt-studio/ui/fonts.css";
import "@/lib/prompt-studio/ui/styles.css";

const sections = [
  { id: "quick-start", label: "Quick start" },
  { id: "brief", label: "1. The brief" },
  { id: "directions", label: "2. Starting directions" },
  { id: "layers", label: "3. Visual language" },
  { id: "fine-tune", label: "4. Fine-tune" },
  { id: "send", label: "5. Send to a model" },
  { id: "copy", label: "6. Copy and compare" },
  { id: "recipes", label: "Recipes and privacy" },
  { id: "tips", label: "Tips" },
];
---

<Base
  title="Guide"
  meta_title="Guide — Visual Prompt Studio"
  description="How to use every part of Visual Prompt Studio: the brief, directions, visual layers, model targets, copying, and saving recipes."
  hideChrome={true}
  pageTheme="dark"
>
  <div id="studio-app">
    <StudioBar variant="page" current="guide" />

    <div class="guide">
      <nav class="guide-toc" aria-label="On this page">
        <h2>On this page</h2>
        <ol>
          {
            sections.map((s) => (
              <li>
                <a href={`#${s.id}`}>{s.label}</a>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ol>
      </nav>

      <article class="guide-body">
        <header class="guide-head">
          <h1 class="lede">How to use the studio.</h1>
          <p>
            Visual Prompt Studio turns an art direction into a prompt for an
            image model. It does not make the image. You compose a recipe, copy
            the prompt, and paste it into ChatGPT Images, Gemini, Midjourney,
            FLUX or Stable Diffusion.
          </p>
        </header>

        <section class="guide-section" id="quick-start" aria-labelledby="qs-h">
          <h2 id="qs-h">Quick start</h2>
          <ol class="guide-steps">
            <li>
              Type what you are making in <strong>What are you making?</strong>,
              for example “poster for a night market in Lisbon”.
            </li>
            <li>
              Pick a <strong>Format</strong> and what the design should do.
            </li>
            <li>
              Choose a card under <strong>Start from a direction</strong>, or
              set Layout, Imagery and Finish yourself.
            </li>
            <li>
              Under <strong>Send to</strong>, pick the model you will use.
            </li>
            <li>
              Press <strong>Copy prompt</strong> and paste it into that model.
            </li>
          </ol>
          <p class="guide-note">
            The prompt on the right updates as you change anything, so you can
            experiment freely and watch what changes.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section" id="brief" aria-labelledby="brief-h">
          <h2 id="brief-h">1. The brief</h2>
          <p>
            The brief is the subject and purpose of the image. It is the only
            thing the studio cannot guess.
          </p>
          <h3>What are you making?</h3>
          <p>
            One or two sentences naming the subject and where the image will be
            used. A concrete subject gives every later choice something to work
            on. An empty brief shows a gentle prompt to describe it.
          </p>
          <h3>Format and goal</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Format</strong> is what the image is for: a poster, flyer, book
            cover, album cover, social post and similar. It sets a matching aspect
            ratio. <strong>What should the design do?</strong> is the communication
            goal. Together they shape which directions the studio recommends first.
          </p>
          <h3>Add text and size</h3>
          <p>This disclosure holds the optional details.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>Aspect ratio</strong>: nine presets such as Feed 4:5 or
              Story 9:16. You can add your own ratio.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Headline</strong> and <strong>Other text</strong>: the
              exact words that should appear. Use one line each for up to four
              extra lines such as a date, venue or credit.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Text in the image</strong>: choose
              <em>Render the exact text</em> to have the model draw your words, or
              <em>Leave clean space</em> to reserve an area and set type yourself
              later.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Your concept</strong>: one memorable visual idea. A
              specific scene beats a list of adjectives.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section
          class="guide-section"
          id="directions"
          aria-labelledby="directions-h"
        >
          <h2 id="directions-h">2. Starting directions</h2>
          <p>
            A direction is a ready-made combination of layout, imagery, finish,
            palette, typeface and intent. It saves you from starting with a
            blank page. Pick one, then change anything.
          </p>
          <h3>Recommended cards</h3>
          <p>
            The cards under <strong>Start from a direction</strong> follow your format
            and goal. Curated picks are studio recommendations, not usage counts.
          </p>
          <h3>2026 starters</h3>
          <p>
            Six contemporary directions are marked as 2026 starters. Each one
            explains the design principle behind it, the tradeoff it makes, and
            links to supporting reading. These draw on editorial forecasts and
            practitioner examples. They are a starting point, not proof that a
            style performs better.
          </p>
          <h3>What applying a direction changes</h3>
          <p>
            Applying a direction changes layout, imagery, finish, palette,
            typeface, type weight and italics, and creative intent. It keeps
            your brief, exact text, concept, format, aspect ratio, text mode,
            exclusions, and every output setting. You can undo it, and each
            control stays editable.
          </p>
          <h3>Explore all directions</h3>
          <p>
            Open <strong>Explore all directions</strong> to search by name, style
            or goal, filter by project type, goal or style layer, group the results,
            or limit them to curated picks or 2026 starters. The filters combine.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section" id="layers" aria-labelledby="layers-h">
          <h2 id="layers-h">3. Visual language</h2>
          <p>
            The look is split into three independent layers, so you combine them
            instead of choosing one flat style name. Each is colour-coded like a
            print separation wherever it appears.
          </p>
          <h3><i class="sep c" aria-hidden="true"></i>Layout</h3>
          <p>
            How the page is organised: grid, hierarchy, where the type sits.
            Examples: Swiss International, Bauhaus, Art Deco.
          </p>
          <h3><i class="sep m" aria-hidden="true"></i>Imagery</h3>
          <p>
            The art movement or visual language of the picture itself. Examples:
            Surrealism, Pop Art, Ukiyo-e.
          </p>
          <h3><i class="sep y" aria-hidden="true"></i>Finish</h3>
          <p>
            The print or material treatment. Examples: Risograph, Letterpress,
            Cyanotype.
          </p>
          <p>
            Switch between the three tabs, pick one style in each, and watch the
            proof sheet on the right. The proof is a vector study of the
            direction, not a generated image. Its swatch list shows what each
            layer contributes.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section" id="fine-tune" aria-labelledby="fine-h">
          <h2 id="fine-h">4. Fine-tune the direction</h2>
          <p>
            Open <strong>Fine-tune the direction</strong> when the layers are right
            but the result needs more control.
          </p>
          <h3>Typography</h3>
          <p>
            Preview typefaces and choose one. The model receives a description
            of the type’s character, not the font file, so add exact lettering
            in your image editor afterwards if you need it.
          </p>
          <h3>Remove the default look</h3>
          <p>
            Select the clichés you want to avoid. Some exclusions depend on the
            style you chose; those are called out and skipped automatically. Use
            the links to apply sensible defaults or clear the list.
          </p>
          <h3>Authorial intent</h3>
          <p>
            Optional. Describe the point of view you want the image to take,
            such as the feeling or the argument behind it.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section" id="send" aria-labelledby="send-h">
          <h2 id="send-h">5. Send to a model</h2>
          <p>
            The prompt’s structure changes with the target model. Choose it
            under <strong>Send to</strong>.
          </p>
          <div class="guide-table-wrap">
            <table class="guide-table">
              <thead>
                <tr><th>Target</th><th>What to expect</th></tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td>ChatGPT Images</td>
                  <td>
                    Natural language. Strong text rendering; every word that
                    must appear is quoted.
                  </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Gemini</td>
                  <td>
                    Natural language, like briefing a designer. Every word that
                    must appear is quoted; refine in the same chat.
                  </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Midjourney</td>
                  <td>
                    A descriptive phrase with parameters at the end: <code
                      >--ar</code
                    >, <code>--no</code>, <code>--sref</code>, <code>--raw</code
                    >. Only the headline is sent.
                  </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>FLUX</td>
                  <td>
                    No negative prompts. Exclusions are rewritten as what you
                    want instead.
                  </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Stable Diffusion</td>
                  <td>
                    Separate positive and negative prompts. Weak at text, so
                    prefer leaving clean space.
                  </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Other model</td>
                  <td>Plain language with constraints stated in words.</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <h3>Prompt shape</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Art direction</strong> is one dense paragraph that works everywhere.
            <strong>Seven-line brief</strong> keeps the same seven lines every time
            so you can swap only the style and compare.
            <strong>Production spec</strong> is a sectioned brief for complex layouts
            and handoff. <strong>Point-of-view brief</strong> explains what each section
            controls. Which shapes are offered depends on the model.
          </p>
          <h3>Restyle and edit</h3>
          <p>
            For ChatGPT Images, <strong>Restyle in same chat</strong> forces a clean
            restart so the previous image does not leak in.
            <strong>Edit with reference</strong> changes one thing in an attached
            image: fill in <strong>Change only</strong> and
            <strong>Keep exactly</strong> to protect the rest.
          </p>
          <h3>Midjourney settings</h3>
          <p>
            With Midjourney selected you can add style reference codes (<code
              >--sref</code
            >), switch on <code>--raw</code> to reduce the model’s default beautification,
            and set <code>--s</code> stylize. Leave stylize blank to use the default.
          </p>
          <h3>Language</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>EN</strong> or <strong>ES</strong> sets the language of the generated
            prompt. It does not translate the text you want in the image.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section" id="copy" aria-labelledby="copy-h">
          <h2 id="copy-h">6. Copy and compare</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>Copy prompt</strong> copies the full prompt. The small icon
              in the prompt box does the same.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Open in ChatGPT</strong> appears for the GPT Image target and
              opens ChatGPT with the prompt filled in. Prompts sent this way travel
              in the link, so they can show up in your browser history.
              <strong>Copy &amp; open</strong> appears for Gemini, Midjourney and
              FLUX. These apps cannot be prefilled from a link, so the button copies
              the prompt and opens the app for you to paste it. Stable Diffusion and
              Other model have no button.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Copy negative</strong> appears for models that use a separate
              negative prompt.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Compare with an undirected prompt</strong> shows what the model
              would get from your brief alone, so you can see what the art direction
              adds.
            </li>
            <li>
              Notices under the prompt flag anything the target model handles
              differently, such as skipped exclusions or text limits.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p class="guide-note">
            The studio never generates the image. Paste the prompt into your
            image tool and iterate from there.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section" id="recipes" aria-labelledby="rec-h">
          <h2 id="rec-h">Recipes and privacy</h2>
          <p>
            Everything you choose is your <strong>recipe</strong>. It saves in
            your browser automatically, so it is still there when you return.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>Export</strong> shows the recipe as JSON to copy or download.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Import</strong> restores a recipe from pasted JSON or a file.
              It is validated first, and problems are listed before anything changes.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Reset</strong> returns every choice to its default.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The studio makes no API calls and sends no recipe data to a server.
            Clearing your browser’s site data removes the saved recipe, so
            export anything you want to keep.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section" id="tips" aria-labelledby="tips-h">
          <h2 id="tips-h">Tips</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Use the <strong>info</strong> buttons beside any field for a short explanation
              of what it does.
            </li>
            <li>Change one layer at a time to see what each contributes.</li>
            <li>
              Use <em>Leave clean space</em> when the text must be perfect, and set
              it in a layout tool.
            </li>
            <li>
              The <a href="/#inspiration">Where to look next</a> section links to
              sources for styles, style codes and tested prompts.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <aside class="guide-cta" aria-label="Open the studio">
          <p>Ready to compose?</p>
          <a class="btn primary" href="/#compose">Open the studio</a>
        </aside>
      </article>
    </div>

    <SiteFooter variant="studio" />
  </div>
</Base>
